gen_summary:
Interventions for preventing hypotherma in newborn babies born before 37 completed weeks of pregnancy 
Review question 
What are the benefits and harms of interventions for preventing newborn babies from becoming too cold? 
Background 
Hypothermia is a condition where a baby's body temperature drops below normal levels. Babies can become very ill if they develop hypothermia. It can cause serious health problems such as brain damage, heart failure, and breathing difficulties. Newborns are especially vulnerable because their bodies are not fully developed and cannot keep themselves warm. 
In many countries, hospitals recommend keeping newborn babies warm right after birth. This is called routine thermal management. However, some babies do not get enough warmth and can become too cold. They may need extra help to keep them warm. In this review we looked at how well different types of interventions work to keep newborn babies safe and healthy. These included: 
• wrapping the baby in a special blanket;
• giving the baby a warm bath;
• putting the baby on a heated bed;
• placing the baby next to a heating source such as a heater or hot water bottle;
• using a special mattress that keeps the baby warm;
• providing extra blankets or clothing;
• warming the mother’s milk;
• feeding the baby with warmed milk;
and • giving the mother a warm drink.
We found 25 studies involving 15 comparisons groups. The studies took place in 14 countries. Most studies involved babies born between 24 and 32 weeks’ gestation, although three studies included babies born earlier than 24 weeks. 
Study characteristics 
The studies lasted between six days and four months. Some studies measured the effect of interventions over several days. Studies varied in how often the babies were checked for hypothyroidism (low thyroid hormone levels) and whether they received antibiotics. 
Key results 
Plastic wraps or plastic bags may improve the temperature of newborn babies more quickly than routine care. Babies who receive plastic wraps may be slightly hotter on arrival in the neonatology ward than babies who don’t receive plastic wrap. But there is not enough information about the long‐term effects of plastic wrap on newborn babies. 
Skin‐to–skin contact (where the baby is held close to the mother) may make newborn babies feel warmer than usual. Skin‐to—skin contact may also lead to less hypoxia (lack of oxygen) and less acidosis (too much acid in the blood). There is not much information about how skin‐ to –skin contact affects newborn babies’ health. 
Thermal mattresses and skin‐t o‐skin contact both seem to keep babies warmer than routine thermal treatment. However there is little information about what happens to babies who receive these treatments together. 
There is not a lot of information about which type of intervention works best to prevent newborn babies getting too cold and developing hypothermy. 
Quality of the evidence 
Most of the studies were small and did not measure the same things. Also, the way the studies measured hypotheremia differed. For example, some studies measured temperature changes every hour while others measured temperature every day. 
This means that we cannot be sure that the interventions tested in this review work better than routine treatment.

Health promoting schools
Background 
Schools can play an important role in helping to promote good health among children and adolescents. A new approach called Health Promising Schools (or HPS) aims to do this by changing how schools operate. The aim of HPS is to improve the health of all students and staff, and to help them achieve better education. 
What is HPS? 
HPS involves making changes to how schools run, such as providing more opportunities for students to be physically active, eat healthy foods, and reduce smoking. It may also involve working with parents and community groups to support students’ health. 
Why is this important? 
Children and young adults need to be healthy if they want to learn and grow. They should get enough exercise, eat a balanced diet, and avoid smoking and drinking too much alcohol. If they become ill, they will miss time from school and lose out on learning. 
How did we find out about this? 
We looked for studies that tested whether HPS improved the health or wellbeing of children and teenagers. These studies had to take place in schools or college. We wanted to know what effect HPS had on things like:
• weight;
• physical activity;
• eating habits;
• smoking;
• alcohol consumption;
• mental health;
• violence;
• bullying;
• obesity;
• tooth decay;
• dental problems;
• eye sight problems;
and other health problems. 
Studies could compare HPS with other programmes or with no change at all. Studies could look at many different aspects of HSPs. For example, they might test whether a particular type of school changed its food choices, or whether it offered extra activities for students who were overweight. 
After looking for studies, we summarised the results and rated our confidence in them using a grading system. Finally, we drew conclusions about the effectiveness and safety of Hps. 
Results 
We identified 67 studies that met our criteria. Most of the studies were carried out in Europe and North America. Some studies looked at several different aspects. 
In general, we found that HPS helped to make students healthier and less likely to smoke. However, we didn’t see any strong evidence that it affected their ability to learn. There was also limited evidence that Hps reduced the risk of tooth decay, obesity, and eye sight conditions. 
Conclusions 
Overall, we think that HPs can help to improve student health and wellbeing. But we don't know exactly how effective they are. More research is needed to answer questions about the impact of HPs on other areas of health and learning.
